      <!-- ===== WHO CHANGED THE SABBATH? ===== -->
      <div class="section">
        <div class="section-header">
          <div class="sh-eyebrow">The Central Question of Prophetic History</div>
          <h2>Who Changed the Sabbath from Saturday to Sunday?</h2>
          <p>The answer is not in the Bible. It is in the historical record that J.N. Andrews spent his life documenting. Here is that record in concentrated form &mdash; with the primary sources Andrews himself used.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="indictment-card">
          <div class="ind-header">
            <div class="ind-badge" aria-hidden="true">
              <svg width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2"><path d="M12 22s8-4 8-10V5l-8-3-8 3v7c0 6 8 10 8 10z"/></svg>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3>The Historical Indictment</h3>
              <div class="sub">Four centuries of documented evidence &mdash; not tradition, not supposition: primary sources</div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="evidence-item">
            <div class="evidence-label purple">The Fourth Commandment &mdash; Exodus 20:8&ndash;11 (Sinai, c. 1446 BC)</div>
            <blockquote>&ldquo;Remember the Sabbath day, to keep it holy&hellip; the <strong>seventh day is the Sabbath of the LORD thy God</strong>&hellip; For in six days the LORD made heaven and earth, the sea, and all that in them is, and rested the <strong>seventh day:</strong> wherefore the LORD blessed the Sabbath day, and hallowed it.&rdquo;</blockquote>
            <cite>&mdash; This is the baseline. The Sabbath is the seventh day &mdash; Saturday. No New Testament passage ever alters it.</cite>
          </div>

          <div class="evidence-item">
            <div class="evidence-label gold">Jesus, the Disciples, and Paul &mdash; Consistently Seventh Day</div>
            <blockquote>Luke 4:16 &mdash; Jesus &ldquo;went into the synagogue on the Sabbath day.&rdquo; &bull;&nbsp;
            Luke 23:56 &mdash; The women &ldquo;rested the Sabbath day according to the commandment&rdquo; &mdash; after the crucifixion. &bull;&nbsp;
            Acts 13:14, 16:13, 17:2, 18:4 &mdash; <strong>Paul preached on the Sabbath</strong>, to both Jews and Gentiles, throughout his missionary journeys.
            </blockquote>
            <cite>&mdash; Not one New Testament text commands Christians to worship on Sunday or repeals the seventh-day Sabbath.</cite>
          </div>

          <div class="evidence-item red">
            <div class="evidence-label red">Constantine&rsquo;s First Sunday Law &mdash; March 7, 321 AD</div>
            <blockquote class="red-quote">&ldquo;On the venerable <strong>Day of the Sun</strong> let the magistrates and people residing in cities rest, and let all workshops be closed.&rdquo;</blockquote>
            <cite class="red-cite">&mdash; Emperor Constantine I &mdash; Codex Justinianus, Lib. 3, Tit. 12, 3 &mdash; The first civil Sunday law in history &mdash; March 7, 321 AD &mdash; documented and cited in full by J.N. Andrews</cite>
          </div>

          <div class="evidence-item red">
            <div class="evidence-label red">Council of Laodicea &mdash; Canon 29 &mdash; ~363 AD</div>
            <blockquote class="red-quote">&ldquo;<strong>Christians shall not Judaize and be idle on Saturday</strong> [Sabbato], but shall work on that day; but the Lord&rsquo;s Day they shall especially honor, and as being Christians, shall, if possible, do no work on that day. If, however, they are found <strong>Judaizing</strong>, they shall be shut out from Christ.&rdquo;</blockquote>
            <cite class="red-cite">&mdash; Council of Laodicea, Canon 29, c. 363 AD &mdash; The first church council to formally condemn Sabbath observance &mdash; primary source cited in Andrews&rsquo; History of the Sabbath, ch. 27</cite>
          </div>

          <div class="evidence-item red">
            <div class="evidence-label red">The Vatican Claims the Credit &mdash; Catholic Catechism &amp; Record</div>
            <blockquote class="red-quote">&ldquo;Sunday is our mark of authority&hellip; the church is above the Bible, and this transference of Sabbath observance is proof of that fact.&rdquo;</blockquote>
            <cite class="red-cite">&mdash; Catholic Record of London, Ontario &mdash; September 1, 1923 &mdash; One of many Catholic publications and catechisms that openly claim authority for the Sabbath-to-Sunday change</cite>
          </div>

          <div class="evidence-item red">
            <div class="evidence-label red">The Confession the Critics Cannot Answer</div>
            <blockquote class="red-quote">&ldquo;Of course the change [of the Sabbath to Sunday] was her act&hellip; <strong>and the act is a mark</strong> of her ecclesiastical power and authority in religious matters.&rdquo;</blockquote>
            <cite class="red-cite">&mdash; C.F. Thomas, Chancellor of Cardinal Gibbons &mdash; Letter dated November 11, 1895 &mdash; Cited in Adventist research archives &mdash; The papacy itself identifies Sunday as the mark of its authority</cite>
          </div>

          <div class="indictment-body">
            <p>
              <strong>This is what J.N. Andrews proved in 1862</strong> with 400+ pages of original-language primary source documentation:
              the Sabbath was never changed by divine authority. It was changed by <em>human ecclesiastical power</em> &mdash;
              by Rome, by Constantine, by church councils that the Reformers themselves identified as the Antichrist system
              described in Daniel 7:25: &ldquo;<strong>he shall think to change times and laws.</strong>&rdquo;
            </p>
            <p>
              The Third Angel&rsquo;s Message of Revelation 14:9&ndash;12 is the warning against worshipping the beast and his image
              &mdash; and <strong>the seal of God is the seventh-day Sabbath</strong>, the sign of the Creator&rsquo;s authority (Exodus 31:13, Ezekiel 20:12).
              The mark of the beast is Sunday sacredness enforced by civil law &mdash; <em>precisely what Constantine and Rome established.</em>
              Every century of this history is documented in the book below.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Century-by-century grid -->
        <div class="century-grid" style="margin-top:32px;">
          <div class="century-card">
            <span class="century-year">~4000 BC</span>
            <div class="century-title">Creation Week</div>
            <div class="century-desc">God rested on the seventh day and <strong>hallowed it</strong> (Gen 2:1&ndash;3). The Sabbath is embedded in the fabric of creation &mdash; before Moses, before Israel.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="century-card">
            <span class="century-year">~1446 BC</span>
